-- How a `Declares` says what it extends, and how `specifies:` types the parts
-- of a destructuring target.


-- The `Declares:` target may state the type this definition extends. `via X`
-- names the view used to regard a magma as a set, and types `X` at the same
-- time, so `specifies:` only has to type what the `via` does not reach.
[\magma]
Declares: M ::= (X, *) is \set via X
specifies: * is \operation:on{M}
Documented:
. called: "magma"
Id: "0e5be05c-2b6d-48cc-8dec-d656eb6bb8b7"


-- A second component, typed in `specifies:`. `X` is not repeated: a symbol is
-- specified exactly once.
[\monoid]
Declares: M ::= (X, *, e) is \magma via (X, *)
specifies: e "in" X
Documented:
. called: "monoid"
Id: "b41b952a-239a-49de-b27a-a896f0494b30"


-- A `via` tuple maps positionally onto the extended type's own components, so
-- `X`, `*` and `e` all inherit their types here and no `specifies:` is needed.
[\group]
Declares: G ::= (X, *, e) is \monoid via (X, *, e)
Documented:
. called: "group"
Id: "bc7fdaea-a45a-4569-8607-78555d8a03eb"


-- `extends:` is the same clause written as a section. It exists for what a
-- target cannot express: extending several types at once, each through a
-- different view of the tuple.
[\bimagma]
Declares: B ::= (X, *, +)
extends:
. B is \magma via (X, *)
. B is \magma via (X, +)
Documented:
. called: "bimagma"
Id: "fde4a779-17a4-48a3-997b-ad20380eb324"


-- Subtyping is recursive, so a group is a magma and therefore a set.
Theorem:
given: G ::= (X, *, e) is \group
then:
. G is? \monoid
. G is? \magma
. G is? \set
. X is? \set
. e "in"? X
Documented:
. called: "a group is a set"
Id: "da7562a6-f7e7-4e0e-ba84-30ed132faeb1"
